1: # Independent audit of the default negative-fan source-zero prisms
2: 
3: This is a separate working audit of the explicit local cone collections
4: in `notes/part3_source_zero_prism_working.md`. It does not promote a
5: global K39 filling or assign a source-loop return. The calculation uses
6: only the current explicit matrices, rays, and staircase tetrahedra.
7: 
8: The default I-boundary marking is the negative diagonal, with
9: \(u=e_1,v=-e_2\) in the I and E sectors. The earlier positive-diagonal
10: variant is a valid separate fan construction and is preserved completely
11: in `notes/part3_source_zero_prism_positive_audit.md` and the matching
12: `source_zero_prism_positive_audit` certificate files. The change here
13: retains the actual default boundary marking, not a silent identification
14: of the two fans.
15: 
16: The degree/support argument passes. In addition, the noncollapsed
17: cones, together with all their faces, already form a fan in each of
18: the three sectors. The collapsed cones in either rank-one sector are
19: existing faces of the noncollapsed cones. A periodic common refinement
20: of the three H-boundary fans, with compatible rational integral-sector
21: refinements, is constructed below. The unit action, quotient properness,
22: and analytic cap attachment remain separate steps.
23: 
24: ## PF.1. Exact domain, slice map, and the degree argument
25: 
26: Keep the actual integer pairs \((a,b)=(1,2),(2,1)\), the integer
27: \(N>1\), and
28: \[
29:  I=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\end{pmatrix},\quad
30:  W=\begin{pmatrix}1&b\\-a&-2\end{pmatrix},\quad
31:  E=\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\0&N\end{pmatrix},\quad
32:  H=\begin{pmatrix}2&b\\-a&N-1\end{pmatrix}.
33: \]
34: For each pair \((P_0,H)\), the original rays and action are
35: \[
36:  R_0(n)=(P_0n,1,0),\quad R_1(n)=(Hn,0,1),\quad
37:  T_m(x,r,s)=(x+rP_0m+sHm,r,s).                         \tag{PF.1}
38: \]
39: All geometric inspection changes below leave these integral vectors
40: unchanged; they are explicitly rational linear maps used to inspect
41: their cones.
42: 
43: Use \(u=e_1,v=-e_2\) in the I and E sectors, and
44: \(u=e_1,v=e_2-be_1\) in W. Start with the same staircase cones
45: for \(P_0=P_1=I\). On
46: \(r+s=1\) they are exactly the standard triangulation of
47: \(\mathbb R^2\times[0,1]\). The two unit-square triangles
48: have ordered vertices \((0,u,u+v)\), \((0,v,u+v)\), and
49: the three tetrahedra on each are those in the root note. The common
50: diagonal of every vertical rectangle agrees on adjacent prisms, so
51: this is a face-to-face domain triangulation.
52: 
53: Linearly map its vertices to (PF.1). At height \(s=t\), the
54: map is \(F_t\), with literal periodicity
55: \[
56:  F_t(x+m)=F_t(x)+P(t)m,\qquad P(t)=(1-t)P_0+tH.          \tag{PF.2}
57: \]
58: Its difference from \(P(t)x\) is periodic and bounded. The
59: three determinants, at arbitrary positive \(r,s\), are
60: \[
61:  \begin{array}{ll}
62:  P_0=I:&r^2+(N+1)rs+2Ns^2,\\
63:  P_0=W:&(N-1)rs+2Ns^2,\\
64:  P_0=E:&2Nrs+2Ns^2.
65:  \end{array}                                             \tag{PF.3}
66: \]
67: They are positive. Hence (PF.2) descends to the stated two-torus
68: map and the straight-line periodic homotopy gives degree one.
69: On every noncollapsed slice piece its Jacobian is the signed
70: four-dimensional determinant ratio. These are exactly
71: \[
72:  \begin{array}{c|cc}
73:   & (0,u,u+v)&(0,v,u+v)\\ \hline
74:  (I,H)&(1,N-1,2N)&(1,2,2N)\\
75:  (W,H)&(0,N-1,2N)&(0,0,2N)\\
76:  (E,H)&(0,0,2N)&(0,2N,2N).
77:  \end{array}                                             \tag{PF.4}
78: \]
79: Here each determinant is divided by the determinant of the same
80: domain cone, so its orientation sign is fixed rather than inferred
81: from an absolute determinant.
82: 
83: Outside the finite polygonal images of lower-dimensional and collapsed
84: pieces on the quotient torus, every preimage lies in a noncollapsed
85: piece and has positive local degree. Degree one therefore gives one
86: preimage. It follows that distinct noncollapsed image interiors do
87: not overlap. Their closures cover each slice: their finite union on
88: the quotient is closed and contains the dense set of these regular
89: values. Lifting and using the bounded periodic difference proves full
90: support in the whole plane. This verifies exactly the root note's
91: degree/support statement. It does not claim raw injectivity in a
92: rank-one sector.
93: 
94: ## PF.2. The I,H sector has exact common faces
95: 
96: Every entry in its determinant row is strictly positive. Triangulate
97: the slice parallelograms by a diagonal, without changing their affine
98: maps. In the resulting planar triangulation, an edge cannot fold:
99: the two positively oriented adjacent triangles map to opposite sides
100: of its image. At a vertex, the images of successive positively
101: oriented sectors turn through strictly positive angles less than
102: \(\pi\). Their closed link has positive integral winding, so
103: the local degree is a positive integer. The same is true in an edge
104: or face interior.
105: 
106: The global degree is one. The sum of these positive local degrees
107: over any fibre is consequently one, including values on the images
108: of edges and vertices. Equivalently one can take a small regular
109: value in the link disc. Thus every point has exactly one preimage
110: and local degree one. Properness follows from (PF.2); the slice map
111: is a homeomorphism. The endpoint slice maps are the invertible
112: linear maps \(I\) and \(H\), and the same conclusion holds
113: there.
114: 
115: The full map preserves the two heights and is homogeneous. It is
116: therefore injective on the union of the original cones. For two
117: domain cones \(C,D\),
118: \[
119:                     F(C)\cap F(D)=F(C\cap D).            \tag{PF.5}
120: \]
121: Their original intersection is a common face, and each cone map is
122: linear and invertible. Thus (PF.5) is a common face of the two image
123: cones. This proves the fan property for the I,H sector without an
124: additional refinement.
125: 
126: ## PF.3. A literal strip model for both rank-one sectors
127: 
128: For W use the prescribed basis
129: \(u=e_1,v=e_2-be_1\). Then \(P_0v=0\). For E exchange the
130: two prescribed basis vectors, so \(u=-e_2,v=e_1\), and again \(P_0v=0\).
131: Exchanging these vectors only exchanges the two triangles in the
132: given collection; it does not change its cones. Put
133: \[
134:                    p=P_0u,\qquad A=Hu,\qquad B=Hv.
135: \]
136: Apply the rational inspection map with basis \((A,B)\) in the
137: two fibre coordinates. It sends \(A\) to \((1,0)\),
138: \(B\) to \((0,1)\), and \(p\) to \((c,d)\), where
139: \[
140:  \begin{array}{c|cc}
141:        &c&d\\ \hline
142:  W&(N-1)/(2N)&-a/(2N)\\
143:  E&1&+b/2.
144:  \end{array}                                             \tag{PF.6}
145: \]
146: In each case \(c>0\). This map is not declared unimodular;
147: all actual rays remain (PF.1) in their original lattice.
148: 
149: At fixed \(r,s>0\), the two top-triangle cones give precisely
150: the square
151: \[
152:                 Q=[0,s](1,0)+[0,s](0,1),
153: \]
154: split along its diagonal from \((0,0)\) to \((s,s)\).
155: The one noncollapsed mixed cone gives the parallelogram
156: \[
157:            J=(s,0)+[0,r](c,d)+[0,s](0,1).                \tag{PF.7}
158: \]
159: These are the three noncollapsed maximal slice cells of the original
160: prism collection, not a replacement collection. Their union is
161: exactly
162: \[
163:  \begin{split}
164:  0\le x\le L:=s+rc,&\qquad f(x)\le y\le f(x)+s,\\
165:  f(x)&=\begin{cases}
166:        0,&0\le x\le s,\\
167:        (d/c)(x-s),&s\le x\le s+rc.
168:        \end{cases}
169:  \end{split}                                             \tag{PF.8}
170: \]
171: The actual periods in these coordinates are
172: \[
173:                  rP_0u+sHu=(L,rd),\qquad
174:                  rP_0v+sHv=(0,s).                        \tag{PF.9}
175: \]
176: They are exactly the translations used to tile by (PF.8).
177: 
178: Here is an explicit inverse cell assignment. For a point off the
179: displayed boundaries, choose the unique integer \(i\) with
180: \(0<x-iL<L\), then the unique integer \(j\) with
181: \[
182:   f(x-iL)<y-i rd-js<f(x-iL)+s.
183: \]
184: The point belongs to the cell labelled \(iu+jv\). Within that
185: cell, \(x-iL<s\) places it in Q, where the single diagonal
186: chooses one of the two triangles. For \(x-iL>s\), its actual
187: parallelogram coordinates are
188: \[
189:  \alpha=\frac{x-iL-s}{rc},\qquad
190:  \beta=\frac{y-i rd-js-(d/c)(x-iL-s)}s,
191:                  \qquad 0<\alpha,\beta<1.                \tag{PF.10}
192: \]
193: This proves both uniqueness and coverage with the exact period maps.
194: 
195: All boundary identifications in this tiling are whole faces. The
196: square and J share their full vertical edge. The right edge of J
197: is the left edge of the next square, under \((L,rd)\).
198: The top piecewise-linear boundary is the bottom boundary translated
199: by \((0,s)\), and the break at \(x=s\) occurs at exactly the
200: same place on both sides. The square's diagonal meets these edges
201: only at its two endpoints. Thus no T-junction or partial-edge
202: intersection remains. This explicitly proves common faces for all
203: positive height ratios, not merely disjoint interiors.
204: 
205: The ray identifications are independent of \(r,s\). Writing
206: the cell label as zero, the two collapsed bottom tetrahedra both
207: have the image face
208: \[
209:        \operatorname{cone}(R_0(0),R_0(u),R_1(u+v)),        \tag{PF.11}
210: \]
211: because \(R_0(v)=R_0(0)\) and
212: \(R_0(u+v)=R_0(u)\). This is the upper face of J and the
213: lower face of its v-translate. The other collapsed mixed tetrahedron
214: has the image face
215: \[
216:        \operatorname{cone}(R_0(0),R_1(v),R_1(u+v)),        \tag{PF.12}
217: \]
218: the top edge of Q and the bottom edge of its v-translate. Equations
219: (PF.11)--(PF.12) show that omitting the collapsed maximal cones and
220: then adjoining the ordinary faces loses no ray or required face.
221: 
222: The remaining face incidences are also literal common-ray subsets:
223: the diagonal of Q uses \(R_0(0),R_1(0),R_1(u+v)\);
224: the Q/J edge uses \(R_0(0),R_1(u),R_1(u+v)\);
225: the J/next-Q edge uses \(R_0(u),R_1(u),R_1(u+v)\).
226: Together with their period translates they give every interior
227: facet. The rank-one base face has just the rays \(R_0(iu)\)
228: and intervals between consecutive such rays; the other base face
229: has the ordinary H-triangle fan. These are faces of the same cones.
230: This completes the common-face proof, including the height boundary.
231: 
232: ## PF.4. A compatible periodic refinement on the common H boundary
233: 
234: Apply \(H^{-1}\) on the H-height-one slice, solely to describe
235: its geometry. The I and E sectors give the negative-diagonal unit-square
236: triangulation \(\mathcal T_-\), with basis \(e_1,-e_2\); W gives
237: \(\mathcal T_b\), with basis \(e_1,e_2-be_1\). The latter
238: is the shear of the positive-diagonal square triangulation, not the
239: shear of \(\mathcal T_-\). For \(b=1\) it already equals
240: \(\mathcal T_-\). Both are
241: \(\mathbb Z^2\)-periodic, locally finite, rational triangulations.
242: Take their literal overlay
243: \[
244:        \mathcal P=\{\sigma\cap\tau:
245:                    \sigma\in\mathcal T_-,\ 
246:                    \tau\in\mathcal T_b\},              \tag{PF.13}
247: \]
248: including all its faces and omitting empty intersections. This is
249: an exact common polyhedral refinement. Indeed a face of a member
250: is obtained by an active supporting inequality from one of its
251: two factors; intersecting two members activates the corresponding
252: common faces in the two original complexes. Their intersection is
253: therefore a common face. Local finiteness follows because a compact
254: set meets finitely many triangles in each factor. The periodic
255: quotient has finitely many cells, and every vertex is rational.
256: 
257: The certificate computes this overlay with rational arithmetic for
258: both actual b values, on a fundamental square and its neighbouring
259: period translates. It checks area one and every common-face
260: intersection. It retains the rational vertices; they are not silently
261: replaced by lattice vertices.
262: 
263: Coning the cells of \(H\mathcal P\) in the H boundary gives
264: the common rational boundary fan. Replace each rational ray by its
265: primitive integral generator in the original four-dimensional
266: lattice. This is an exact equality of the ray, not a change of the
267: lattice. If a simplicial refinement is wanted, use the barycentre
268: of each polygonal cell and the induced boundary subdivisions,
269: periodically.
270: 
271: For the E sector the other base boundary must also retain the actual
272: source chain. Its raw rays are \((0,Nj,1,0)\). Subdivide them by
273: all the primitive rays
274: \[
275:                          (0,j,1,0),\qquad j\in\mathbb Z.
276:                                                                \tag{PF.14}
277: \]
278: The scalar period sends \(j\) to \(j+N\), exactly as in the
279: actual E-boundary chain; the other kernel period and its unit are
280: retained. If \(\xi_E\) is this primitive base height character
281: and \(V\) the vertical torus character, the adjacent charts are
282: literally
283: \[
284:  X_j=V\xi_E^{-j},\qquad Y_j=\xi_E^{j+1}/V,
285:                          \qquad X_jY_j=\xi_E.             \tag{PF.15}
286: \]
287: The period \(V\mapsto c\xi_E^N V\), with its actual nonzero
288: unit c retained, carries these to the j+N chart by
289: \(X_{j+N}'=cX_j\), \(Y_{j+N}'=c^{-1}Y_j\). Thus this is
290: exactly the stated \(X_jY_j=v\) chain in its primitive base
291: coordinate, rather than only a refinement of an abstract line.
292: There are N ray orbits under that scalar period. All heights in
293: (PF.14) are one; no extra height is inserted on this E boundary.
294: 
295: There is an explicit compatible extension to each sector fan.
296: Prescribe the common subdivision on its H subfan and (PF.14) on
297: its E subfan; keep the I and W base subfans as prescribed. The H
298: and E subfans meet only at the zero cone, so these prescriptions
299: agree on their intersection. Process the remaining original faces
300: in increasing dimension. Once the boundary of a face F is subdivided, choose the
301: interior ray equal to the sum of F's primitive ray generators and
302: cone its subdivided boundary from that ray. On the slice \(r+s=1\)
303: this is the usual radial decomposition of a convex polytope from
304: an interior point: each ray from that point meets the boundary
305: once, so it covers and has common faces. Shared faces were processed
306: first and hence receive the same subdivision from both sides.
307: 
308: Every period map in (PF.1) is unimodular and carries primitive ray
309: generators to primitive ray generators. Thus the chosen sums and
310: the entire inductive construction are period-equivariant. There
311: are finitely many face orbits, so the construction has finitely
312: many subdivision types. This proves compatibility on H and on the actual E chain while
313: retaining the integral period actions and every specified boundary unit. It does not assert
314: that the resulting cones are unimodular; a separate lattice
315: resolution may be applied to them with its own proof.
316: 
317: ## PF.5. Exact audit boundary
318: 
319: The local fan, common-H-refinement, and prescribed E-chain claims above
320: pass the independent geometric audit. Root independently passed PF.1--4
321: and requested the explicit E-boundary refinement (PF.14)--(PF.15), which
322: is retained in the same relative coning construction. The raw rank-one prism map still collapses whole
323: tetrahedra; no injectivity statement about that map is restored.
324: The fans are locally finite over compact subsets of the strictly
325: positive height sector and near the full-rank H face. On a rank-one
326: base face the kernel-period unit and the properness of its analytic
327: quotient remain exactly the separate issue identified in the root
328: note. This audit supplies no omitted filling point, global proper
329: family, link multiplicity, characteristic number, or return class.
330: 
